        "subtitle": "How the whole social life of a community, and the well-being of its members, depend on the way they interpret a particular situation.",
        "text": "A  There were once two small communities that had very different ways of seeing the world. They did have one problem in common, however: both had been hit hard by recession, so that in each town about one hundred people were unemployed. They tried hard to find work, but the situation did not improve.\n\nB  The town councillors of Town A had been brought up to believe that there is always enough work for everyone, if you look hard enough. Comforting themselves with this belief, they could have turned their backs on the problem, except that they were genuinely kind-hearted people. They felt they had to prevent the unemployed from suffering, but their principles told them that if people were given something for nothing, it would ruin their moral character. This made the councillors very unhappy, because they were faced with a horrible dilemma: letting the unemployed starve, or destroying their moral character.\n\nC  After much debate, they finally decided to give the unemployed monthly 'relief payments'. To make sure that these payments were not taken for granted, however, they decided that 'relief' would be made so difficult and humiliating to obtain that nobody would be tempted to go through the process unless it was absolutely necessary. In addition, the moral disapproval of the community would be turned on the people who received the money, so that they would try hard to get 'off relief' and 'regain their self-respect'. The councillors expected those who received payments to be grateful, since they were getting something for nothing, something which they had not worked for.\n\nD  When the plan was put into operation, however, the people receiving relief seemed ungrateful. They resented being cross-examined by the 'relief investigators', who decided whether they should receive money. When they allowed themselves a rare luxury, such as going to the cinema, their neighbours who still had jobs looked at them sourly, as if to say, 'I work hard and pay my taxes just to support lazy people like you in idleness and pleasure.' This attitude made them even more resentful. The relief policy prevented starvation, but to the surprise and disappointment of the councillors, it also led to quarrels, unhappy homes, class hatred and crime.\n\nE  The story of the other community, B-ville, was completely different. One of the councillors explained that unemployment, like sickness and accidents, strikes unexpectedly, whatever the victim's merits. He argued that B-ville's homes, parks and industries had been built partly by the work of the people who were now unemployed. He then suggested that if the work these unemployed people had done for the community could be regarded as a kind of 'insurance premium', the payments now made to them could be regarded as 'insurance claims'. In the end, he persuaded the other councillors.\n\nF  B-ville's 'claims adjusters' had a much easier time than Town A's 'relief investigators'. The relief investigators had been resented as snoopers, because of their rather aggressive way of making their demands. The claims adjusters, however, had no moral lesson to teach, only a business transaction to carry out. They treated their clients with business-like courtesy and found out as much as the relief investigators had, with far less difficulty.\n\nG  The councillors held a civic ceremony, at which the governor of the region presented the first insurance cheques. There were speeches, photographs and cheering. As a result, all the recipients felt that they had been personally honoured, and that they could face their unemployment with greater courage, since their community was behind them. All in all, unlike the unemployed in Town A, B-ville's unemployed were not haunted by a sense of failure, did not turn to crime, did not show personal maladjustments and did not develop class hatred as a result of their monthly payment."
